WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:09.100
In this walkthrough we'll run pre-trade
analysis on a government bond and promote it
to a booking ticket, in the OpenTRMS Workbench.

2
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:20.667
The Bond Deal Workbook is the first item under Trades. It
carries a single deal through its whole life — pre-trade,
booking, approval, settlement and history — as tabs across one panel.

3
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:28.000
Start a new deal. The workbook opens on
the Pre-Trade tab, because the analysis
comes before the booking, not after it.

4
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.567
Find the bond by its ISIN. This one is a five-year US
dollar demo bond maturing in July twenty thirty-one.

5
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:38.333
Enter the notional — ten
million US dollars of face value.

6
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:42.167
Then the price you expect to deal
at, quoted as a percentage of par.

7
00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:48.133
Choose the counterparty. The pre-trade
check will test this trade against the
credit limits for this name specifically.

8
00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:53.667
And the book, which sets both the portfolio the risk
lands in and the limits the trade is measured against.

9
00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:59.100
Set the trade date, and the settlement date — for
a government bond, normally the next business day.

10
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:04.033
With the settlement date entered,
the workbook prices the trade live.

11
00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:16.733
Here is the pre-trade result. The workbook returns a clean price of
ninety-six point nine three, a yield to maturity of five and a half
percent, and the DV01 — the profit or loss for a one basis point move in rates.

12
00:01:16.733 --> 00:01:27.000
The limit status is green, and it names the binding constraint —
counterparty exposure. That is the limit this trade would come
closest to breaching, so it is the one to watch if the size goes up.

13
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:35.933
The Risk tab breaks the same trade down
further — instrument metrics, key rate
exposures, and the effect on the portfolio as a whole.

14
00:01:35.933 --> 00:01:40.467
With the analysis clean,
promote the ticket to booking.

15
00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:51.333
The Deal Detail tab now holds a booking ticket. The instrument
and the economics carry across from the analysis, and the
workbook resolves the bond's coupon, face value and maturity alongside them.

16
00:01:51.333 --> 00:02:01.267
Counterparty and portfolio are not carried over, so confirm
them on the booking ticket. Re-entering them here is deliberate
— the desk you analysed against is not always the one you book to.

17
00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:04.733
Then the portfolio the position will be held in.

18
00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:08.633
The booking ticket is now
complete, and ready to submit for approval.

19
00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:15.133
Submit the trade. It is validated against
limits and standing instructions, then booked.

20
00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:24.767
The Settlement tab shows the cash movements
the deal generated — one row per coupon,
each with its payment date, amount and direction.

21
00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:33.433
The Coupon Schedule is the same story
from the position's point of view —
every future coupon, projected out to maturity.

22
00:02:33.433 --> 00:02:41.767
And History records every change to the
deal, with who made it and when — the
audit trail the deal carries for its whole life.

23
00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:49.933
That completes Bond Pre-Trade and Booking. Every
surface shown here can also be opened from the agent
panel on the right, by asking for it in plain English.
